# Recomputing the audit from frozen records

The prototype audit (3,444 task instances) and the prototype part of the
reconciliation check were produced by `simulation/run_prototype_evidence.py` and
`simulation/run_reconciliation_check.py` against the audited allocation prototype.
The prototype's code is not part of this package. Its outputs are shipped frozen in
`results/`:

- `prototype_evidence_detail.csv`, `prototype_evidence_summary.csv` — the audit records;
- `reconciliation_prototype.csv` — the prototype run with its plan frozen.

The public-record study reads two pseudonymized snapshots, with accounts
replaced by truncated SHA-256 hashes, bots flagged and no comment text:

- `results/public_prs_copilot_agent.json` — every pull request written by
  GitHub's Copilot coding agent and merged into the dotnet, microsoft and Azure
  organizations between 1 July and 14 September 2026, with each pull request's
  commits (author account hashed, merge commits flagged) and the result of the
  checks run on its last commit;
- `results/public_prs_cpython.json` — every pull request merged into
  python/cpython between 1 and 14 September 2026.

`simulation/fetch_public_prs.py` retrieves them again through the `gh` CLI;
`simulation/run_public_pr_check.py` evaluates them and draws the sample of 100
agent approvals that was coded by hand (`results/agent_pr_coding_sample.csv`).
The codes are in `results/agent_pr_codes.csv`, and the agreement and the
clauses' precision and recall against them, with two ablations, in
`results/agent_pr_coding_agreement.csv`. The four claims asked of each agent
pull request are in `results/agent_pr_claims.csv` and
`results/agent_pr_claims_summary.csv`. The coding sheets themselves, which
contain the approvers' account names, are not distributed.

The merge-commit remedy (manuscript Section 4.5) is in
`simulation/run_merge_remedy.py`. For the 260 agent pull requests whose only
commits by people merge the base branch, it checks through the GitHub API that
every file of each merge equals its version in one of the two parents, stores a
snapshot without account names or content (`results/agent_pr_merge_remedy.json`),
and evaluates the narrower claim EC-AG-02m
(`simulation/clauses/agent_pr_merge_check.json`) with the unchanged interpreter
(`results/agent_pr_merge_remedy_summary.csv`). It also writes the readiness
report by organization (`results/agent_pr_readiness_by_org.csv`).

Everything the manuscript reports can be recomputed from these files and the
other scripts, none of which needs the prototype:

```bash
pip install -r replication/requirements-lock.txt
python3 simulation/contract_interpreter.py --records results/prototype_evidence_detail.csv --check --strict
python3 simulation/run_public_pr_check.py
python3 simulation/run_public_pr_check.py --snapshot results/public_prs_copilot_agent.json --prefix agent_pr --coding-sample 100 --codes results/agent_pr_codes.csv --claims simulation/clauses/agent_pr_claims.json --out results
python3 simulation/run_merge_remedy.py   # offline; add --fetch to query the forge again
python3 simulation/make_figures.py
python3 simulation/make_specifier_mockup.py   # mockup of an earlier draft, not used in the manuscript
python3 -m pytest simulation/ -q
shasum -a 256 -c replication/SHA256SUMS
```

- `requirements-lock.txt` — exact versions of the third-party packages and the Python version used.
- `SHA256SUMS` — checksums of the article's scripts, clauses, the public-record snapshots and every result file the manuscript reports.
- `results/*_run_manifest.json` — the lineage of each forge run: the version and checksum of each clause file, a checksum of its bindings, the checksums of the adapter scripts, of the interpreter and of the record snapshots, and the window of the records. Its `run_id` is a checksum of the manifest itself, and every row of the verdict files (`public_pr_verdicts.csv`, `agent_pr_verdicts.csv`, `agent_pr_claims.csv`, `agent_pr_merge_remedy_summary.csv`) carries it. The same inputs always give the same `run_id`.

## Additional checks (Sections 4.1, 4.4, 4.5 and 4.7, added for the IST version)

Three checks on the same pull-request records, none of which needs new coders:

- `simulation/run_baselines.py` compares the review clauses with simpler rules computed from
  the same records, scored against the manual consensus (`results/baseline_rules.csv`).
- `results_holdout/` holds the later window of the same agent and organizations, merged between
  15 September and 20 October 2026 (`public_prs_copilot_agent_holdout.json`, retrieved with
  `simulation/fetch_public_prs.py` in its four stages) and its verdicts in `results_holdout/out/`,
  produced by the unchanged clauses. `simulation/run_holdout_comparison.py` writes
  `results/holdout_comparison.csv`.
- `simulation/run_contradiction_audit.py` rereads, through the `gh` CLI, a seeded random sample of
  100 of the 773 contradicted pull requests with commits by a person and counts the lines changed by
  each kind of account (`results/contradiction_audit.csv`, `contradiction_audit_summary.csv`). The
  shipped file is the result of that run; rerunning it needs network access and may differ if
  repositories have changed.
- Held-out coded sample: `results_holdout/out/agent_pr_coding_sample.csv` is a seeded draw of 150 approvals from the
  later window (at most three per repository, none from the first coded sample; `run_public_pr_check.py --coding-sample 150
  --seed 20261022`). Two coders, independent instances of an AI model, applied the written criterion in
  `results_holdout/coding_instructions_ai.txt` to a text file per pull request built by `simulation/prepare_case_files.py`
  (public comment text, not distributed, no verdicts); their codes are in `results_holdout/out/heldout_codes_ai.csv`
  (the justifications they wrote are not distributed). `simulation/run_heldout_eval.py` scores the frozen clauses and the
  simpler rules against them (`results_holdout/heldout_eval.csv`). The two coders agreed on all 150 cases; they share a model
  and the criterion, so the sample is not an independent human reference.
- `results_devin/` holds a time-stratified sample of 886 merged pull requests of a second agent (Devin),
  25 per day chosen at random among those merged at 12:00 UTC between 15 September and 20 October 2026
  (`fetch_public_prs.py --author app/devin-ai-integration --sample-per-day 25`, seed 20261021, then the
  `--augment`, `--merge-flags` and `--checks` stages). The frozen clauses were run on it unchanged
  (`results_devin/out/`), and `simulation/run_second_agent_comparison.py` writes
  `results_devin/second_agent_comparison.csv` with the comparison and three sensitivity analyses.
  There are no manual codes for this agent.
- `simulation/run_cluster_bootstrap.py` gives the later-window differences with intervals from resampling
  whole repositories (`results/holdout_cluster_bootstrap.csv`).
- `simulation/run_instrumentation_asymmetry.py` counts, per pull request, operations by the agent's account
  and by people (`results/instrumentation_asymmetry_*.csv`); it is descriptive.
- `simulation/test_semantic_properties.py` checks, by exhaustive enumeration over small event universes, four
  properties of the verdict rule: independence of event order, invariance to insufficient events, monotonicity of
  supported verdicts for the existential operators, and precedence of declared contradictions.
- `simulation/run_lumping_analysis.py` counts which different cases of the contract a two-output rule (allow or
  deny) would put in one bucket (`results/lumping_analysis.csv`); it reads existing results.
- `simulation/run_stress_breakeven.py` derives the break-even cost ratio of the stress test from
  `results/domain_stress_test_summary.csv` (`results/stress_breakeven.csv`).
- `simulation/run_outcome_association.py` relates documented review to the result of the checks at
  merge and to commits after the last approval. It is exploratory, the measures are not quality
  outcomes, and the article does not report it.

```bash
python3 simulation/run_baselines.py
python3 simulation/run_public_pr_check.py --snapshot results_holdout/public_prs_copilot_agent_holdout.json --prefix agent_pr --claims simulation/clauses/agent_pr_claims.json --out results_holdout/out
python3 simulation/run_holdout_comparison.py
python3 simulation/run_heldout_eval.py
python3 simulation/run_cluster_bootstrap.py
python3 simulation/run_instrumentation_asymmetry.py
python3 simulation/run_stress_breakeven.py
python3 simulation/run_public_pr_check.py --snapshot results_devin/public_prs_devin.json --prefix agent_pr --claims simulation/clauses/agent_pr_claims.json --out results_devin/out
python3 simulation/run_second_agent_comparison.py
python3 simulation/run_outcome_association.py
python3 simulation/run_contradiction_audit.py    # needs network access and the gh CLI
```

`fetch_public_prs.py --checks` now returns "none" for a pull request whose last commit cannot be
read, instead of stopping; results published before this change are unaffected.
